  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Ensuring Protection and Life-Saving Assistance to Populations Affected by Gangs Violence in Haiti</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Violence by non-state armed actors in the Port-au-Prince metropolitan areas (PAPMA) has become a significant threat to the life and well-being of the Haitian population. It is a complex problem resulting from the confluence of several factors, including a social order that has for long been oriented towards exclusion, small elite control of the economic system, political inability to promote social cohesion, and the consequent increase of gangs&#x2019; control in the capital. Since 2018, Haiti has been suffering from a growing socio-political crisis, culminating with the killing of President Jovenel Moise in July 2021 and with the displacement of almost 20,000 people as a consequence of gang-related violence. Most of the population in the PAPMA live with a high level of vulnerability to many types of threats, including health hazards, environmental hazards, acute forms of poverty, and socio-economic exploitation. 
 
With the aim to ensure protection for displaced people and host families in PAPMA, the International Organization for Migration (IOM) is engaged in a multi-sectoral response including Water, Sanitation and Hygiene, Shelter, Camp Coordination and Camp Management, Displacement Tracking Matrix, Health and Psychosocial Wellbeing.</narrative>
  </description>
